{
  "summary": "This study investigates ultralow-loading iridium-doped hollandite manganese oxide nanowires as efficient acidic oxygen evolution reaction (OER) catalysts. The researchers integrated 3 at% iridium into a manganese dioxide framework, reducing the overpotential at 10 mA cm-2 from 580 mV to 303 mV compared to undoped α-MnO2. The catalyst demonstrates significantly higher iridium mass activity than commercial IrOx. Dissolution measurements and spectroscopic analyses revealed modest material loss (79% Ir, 76% Mn retained after 6 h) with iridium remaining oxidized and structurally stable, while manganese undergoes pH- and potential-dependent reduction and structural disorder.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Ultralow iridium loading (3 at%, <16 µgIr cm-2) in hollandite MnO2 framework reduces OER overpotential from 580 mV to 303 mV at 10 mA cm-2",
    "The catalyst achieves iridium mass activity one order of magnitude higher than commercial IrOx",
    "Modest dissolution (79% Ir, 76% Mn retained after 6 h) with iridium remaining oxidized and structurally stable despite pH- and potential-dependent manganese reduction"
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "This work demonstrates that ultralow iridium loading within a manganese oxide framework can significantly enhance acidic OER catalysis while maintaining structural stability, providing a pathway to more sustainable and efficient catalysts for hydrogen production."
}

{
  "summary": "This study investigates whether brief dietary changes influence acute inflammatory responses and long-term tissue memory during disease onset. Using lean mice with acute type 17 skin inflammation, researchers found that a transient high-fat diet (HFD) exposure within a 4-day window exacerbates disease severity comparably to chronic obesity. HFD outside this window or switching obese mice to low-fat diet during the window reduced inflammation. Mechanistically, HFD rapidly recruits neutrophils, which express Nlrp3 and Il1b, driving pathogenic TH17 cell activation. A chromatin-poised tissue-resident memory T cell population persists post-recovery, leading to exaggerated inflammation upon recurrence despite subsequent low-fat diet.",
  "key_findings": [
    "Transient high-fat diet exposure within a 4-day window exacerbates acute type 17 skin inflammation in lean mice to levels comparable with chronic obesity.",
    "HFD outside the 4-day window or switching obese mice to low-fat diet during this window blunts disease amplification.",
    "Neutrophil-mediated Nlrp3-Il1b signaling licenses pathogenic TH17 cells, with persistent tissue-resident memory T cells causing exaggerated inflammation upon recurrence."
  ],
  "scientific_significance": "The study demonstrates that short-lived dietary states during a narrow temporal window can durably reprogram inflammatory disease trajectories through neutrophil-NLRP3-IL-1β axis activation, revealing a mechanism for dietary influences on long-term immune memory."
}
